 ### [Optimized HAVS (Sorted k-buffer)](4735-optimized-havs-sorted-k-buffer.md)
-Optimized HAVS is a refinement of Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting [Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS)](2918-hardware-assisted-visibility-sorting-havs.md) that maintains the k-buffer in a strictly sorted state (ascending order of fragment distance) to reduce the computational cost of fragment-level visibility sorting. While standard HAVS requires $2k-1$ comparisons to identify the two nearest fragments for compositing, this optimization reduces that requirement to $k$ comparisons per fragment processing step after the buffer is full, thereby improving rendering efficiency for large unstructured grid datasets.
+Optimized HAVS (Sorted k-buffer) is an optimization of the Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS) algorithm that maintains the k-buffer in a sorted state (ascending order of fragment distance) to reduce the computational cost of fragment-level visibility sorting. While standard HAVS requires $2k-1$ comparisons to identify the two nearest fragments for compositing, this method reduces that requirement to $k$ comparisons per fragment after the buffer is full. It is primarily used for simulating accurate radiographs involving attenuation and attenuation-emission cases, leveraging modern graphics hardware capabilities.
+
+### [Radiography of Emissive Objects via HAVS](10257-radiography-of-emissive-objects-via-havs.md)
+This method extends Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS) to perform radiography of emissive objects by solving the full radiative transport equation for multiple energy groups. It differs from standard HAVS by computing the transport equation directly in the fragment shader rather than using a pre-computed lookup table, which avoids errors caused by the large dynamic range of extinction coefficients and source terms. It builds on [Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS)](2918-hardware-assisted-visibility-sorting-havs.md) by adding per-energy-group opacity and source term handling.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Object-space sorting is $O(n + k)$ [S2]. Image-space sorting is $O(1)$ per fragment for fixed $k$, but the constant factor depends on the sorting network depth. Total comparisons per pixel for unsorted k-buffer is $N_1 = \sum_{j=1}^{m \times n} (\sum_{l=2}^k (2l-3) + (k+k-1) \times (s_j - k))$ [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low memory overhead as it requires no topological information [S3, unofficial]. Memory usage is dominated by the k-buffer (small) and the pre-integrated transfer function ($128^3$) [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On an ATI Radeon 9800 with $k=2$, HAVS achieved 6.8 fps (1622K tets/sec) for the F117 dataset [S3, unofficial]. With $k=6$, performance dropped to 4.42 fps (1062K tets/sec) for the same dataset [S3, unofficial]. Total performance (CPU+GPU) for F117 was 148ms [S3, unofficial]. With early ray termination, over 6 million cells/sec were achieved [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation (<200 lines of rendering code) [S3, unofficial], handles arbitrary non-convex meshes, no preprocessing required, interactive transfer function updates [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Sorting errors can cause artifacts [S1]. Performance degrades with larger $k$ or highly unsorted fragments [S2]. Requires specific GPU hardware features for read/write framebuffer [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Object-space sorting is $O(n+k)$ using radix/counting sort, where $n$ is the number of primitives and $k$ is the buffer size [S2]. Image-space sorting is $O(1)$ per fragment relative to $k$ (fixed depth sorting network), but the constant factor depends on $k$ [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Low memory overhead as it requires no topological information [S3, unofficial][S8]. Memory usage is dominated by the k-buffer (per-pixel) and the pre-integrated table ($128^3$) [S3, unofficial][S8].
+*   **Performance:** On an ATI Radeon 9800 with a $512^2$ viewport and $k=2$, HAVS achieved up to 9.71 FPS (2331K tets/sec) for the F117 dataset (240,122 cells) [S3, unofficial]. With $k=6$, performance dropped to 4.42 FPS (1062K tets/sec) for the same dataset [S3, unofficial]. Total time (CPU+GPU) for F117 was 148ms [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles arbitrary non-convex meshes with low memory overhead; simpler and more robust than PT/VO or ray casting; no preprocessing required; supports dynamic LOD and transfer function changes [S3, S4, unofficial][S8].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires hardware support for read/write framebuffers; sorting errors can cause artifacts if fragments are not nearly sorted; limited by k-buffer size for highly complex scenes [S1, S2][S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-HAVS was introduced by Steven Callahan, Milan Ikits, Joao Comba, and Claudio Silva in 2005 [S3, S4, unofficial]. It was presented as a novel algorithm for volume rendering unstructured data sets, leveraging the concurrent processing power of CPU and GPU. The method was highlighted as the best compromise in implementation complexity, robustness, and image quality among surveyed techniques at the time [S4, S5, unofficial]. It is used in the HAVS system for rendering large unstructured grids and has been extended to isosurface extraction and dynamic LOD [S3, unofficial].
+HAVS was introduced by Steven Callahan, Milan Ikits, Joao Comba, and Claudio Silva in 2005 [S3, S4, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of previous unstructured volume rendering techniques, which either required complex preprocessing (like PT/VO) or suffered from poor performance on GPUs. HAVS leverages the parallelism of modern GPUs by shifting the heavy lifting of fragment sorting to the GPU while using the CPU for a quick, approximate object-space sort [S3, unofficial][S8]. It is considered one of the most important unstructured grid volume rendering algorithm frameworks based on GPU [S2].
 
